{
 "id": "M06",
 "kind": "echarts",
 "title": "Splitting the audit budget across monitors buys very little",
 "section": "appendix",
 "order": 140,
 "caption": "One budget, spent two ways. The dashed line is the best a single cutoff can do; the solid line is the best an OR of up to this many monitors can do for the same honest false positives in total. Use the dial to change how many you may buy.",
 "notes": [
  "The most any ensemble buys anywhere on this grid is 2.4 points, and at the tightest budget it buys nothing at all: 0.5% is too little to split.",
  "This is an upper bound, not a protocol. Both the monitors and their cutoffs are chosen knowing which programs are backdoored, so the curve is fitted on the data it is scored on. A real ensemble picked in advance would do worse.",
  "The union's false-positive rate is measured, not added: every arm scored the same 500 honest programs, so overlapping alarms are counted once. That is what makes the comparison fair.",
  "The pool is one arm per monitor family, the 0 to 10 stripped arm with the best separation: Sonnet 5.5 UM, Haiku 5.5, GLM-5.3, Qwen 3.8 Omni Flash, gpt-oss-120b, Gemma 3 27B.",
  "Rebuilt from committed per-sample tables, replacing the exported snapshot."
 ],
